{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner independently read and checked the checker’s exact 624 UI report (8df737b8), full 61-line same-object fixture, command runner and commands, both complete root compiler logs, actual JSON failure messages, browser log and restoration record. All 37 frozen manifest entries match their lengths and SHA-256 values; manifest SHA-256 is 8692f63b731261e43741c3433182c5d0f0c640549fcffc6de345e40fc1abd4ed. Both repair paths match immutable 624 blobs and the clone is clean at 624. The final full root compilation finished successfully before runtime with the private fixture included. Actual UI JSON contains 273 passes, one named failure with two AssertionErrors, and no skips. This corroborates the bounded P2 closure in 916ac43d: the lower-activation errors remain repaired, all 28 prior frozen controls and all 243 supplied cases pass, and the unchanged whole browser suite passes 9/9.\n\nThe separate P3 is a valid availability issue for a custom handle that reuses an equal readonly Catalogue. The fixture reads the actual MemoryRoom on every successful call and interns only a byte-equivalent current result. A later successful read of confirmed activation 2 then shares the earlier request’s captured object; confirm’s reference comparison cannot distinguish that completed success and the earlier error clears it. The fresh-object control and honest-null control pass. I independently read immutable LiveRoom confirm, the public acts contract and the built-in client/HTTP read path: the contract does not promise new object identity, while built-in HTTP normally parses fresh response objects. I accept this limited custom-handle finding under the existing Stage 5 promise. Preserving a completed-observation marker rather than relying on reference identity is a possible repair, with the retained activation and honest-null controls preserved.\n\nThis accepts checker evidence, not an additional planner runtime experiment. It establishes no built-in HTTP regression